# Type interpretation

[[type-system]] says what refinement is: a value's structure becomes better
known and the value is never touched. This card says how a program asks, and
what it may ask about: a tree someone authored, a document whose header is
that tree, a card the vault built from a document, and any type a program
declares over one of them. There are three ways to ask and one way to assert,
and they share a single test.

## The test

Every value was built as something. A leaf read out of YAML was built as
`Data`; a literal `1` as an `Int`; a document as a `Doc` or an `HmdDoc`; a card
the vault checked at load as a `ConceptCard` or a `RelationCard`. Asking whether
a value *is* a type `U` means:

- for a declared value, that `U` is what it was built as or an ancestor of it,
  by `:` — see [[type-system]];
- for a `Data` value, that the tree has the shape `U` declares: every required
  path is present and each leaf reads as the leaf type the schema names — see
  [[fields]] for partial schemas.

The first is nominal, the second structural, and which one applies follows from
what the value is, not from a flag at the call site. The outcome is the same
either way: the value is unchanged, and from that point the checker knows more
about it. A `type:` entry in a header is never consulted; it is an author's
claim, and interpretation establishes the type from the contract the claim
points at — see [[card-type]].

## What a program declares

A type over a tree is a partial schema. It names the paths it knows and leaves
the rest open:

```hql
type Book {
    title  : String
    pages  : Int
    genre? : String
}
```

A type over a document is the same thing one level up: a `Doc` whose header has
a shape, declared the way [[fields]] declares `HmdDoc`:

```hql
type RecipeHeader {
    servings    : Int
    ingredients : List<String>
}

type Recipe : HmdDoc {
    header : RecipeHeader
}
```

A card's kinds are declared by the vault extension in exactly this way, and
checked when the vault loads. Nothing about `ConceptCard` is special beyond
that the interpretation already happened — see [[card-type]].

## Asking about one value: `match`

A type is a case, and [[match-op|match]] takes a value apart by its cases. An
arm may name a type, and binds the value under that type. Over a `Data` tree
the arm tests shape:

```hql
match [[dune]].metadata {
    Book b => b.pages,
    _      => 0,
}
```

Over a document it tests the header's shape, since that is what `Recipe` adds
to `HmdDoc`:

```hql
match [[bread]] {
    Recipe r => r.header.servings,
    HmdDoc _ => 1,
}
```

Over a card it tests what the vault established:

```hql
match c {
    RelationCard r => edge(r.source, r.target),
    ConceptCard k  => node(k),
    Card _         => none,
}
```

In every case the arm fires because the shape or the contract holds, never
because a header says `type: Book`. The match is exhaustive over the declared
family, so forgetting the last arm is a type error, the same way a forgotten
`none` is. A value that is not the type takes the other arm. Nothing has failed,
and nothing is reported.

## The test as a value: `is`

`is(U)` is the test itself, returned as a `Bool`: `true` when the value is a
`U` by the test above, `false` otherwise. It is the two-arm `match` with the
arms fixed, the way `sum` is a `reduce` with the step fixed:

```hql
doc | is(Card)

match doc {
    Card _ => true,
    _      => false,
}
```

`is` answers a question and narrows nothing: after `doc | is(Card)` the value
is still a `Doc` to the checker. It belongs in a condition,
`"card" given is(doc, Card) otherwise "document"`. The checker refuses an `is`
that cannot be true, `1 | is(Card)`, and warns about one that cannot be false,
`card | is(Doc)`.

## Asking about many values: `of_type`

`of_type(U)` keeps the values that pass the test and drops the rest. It is a
`filter` whose predicate is the test:

```hql
of_type(U) := filter(x => x | is(U))
```

A `filter` keeps its input's element type for any other predicate. A
predicate of exactly the form `x => x | is(U)` is the one case the checker
reads: the result has element type `U`. The shape of the input is kept, a
`Set` stays a `Set` and a `List` a `List`, and an `Option<T>` becomes an
`Option<U>`. `library` below is a supplied collection of documents.

```hql
cards | map(c => c.metadata) | of_type(Book)      // List<Book>
library | of_type(Recipe)                         // the recipes
cards | of_type(RelationCard) | graph              // Set<RelationCard>
[1, "owl", 2] | of_type(Int)                      // [1, 2]
some(doc) | of_type(Card)                         // Option<Card>
```

The result is the collection itself, not a collection of options, so nothing
is unwrapped after it.

Dropping is the feature. A vault is usable while it is still being typed, and
a tree that satisfies nothing beyond `Data` is still a tree. The checker
refuses an `of_type` only when nothing could pass it: `of_type(Int)` on a
`Set<Card>` selects nothing and is an error at check time.

The first program is where the two tests meet. Each tree was built as `Data`,
so the nominal test says none of them is a `Book`; the structural test says
every tree with a `title` and an integer `pages` is. The structural test is the
one `Data` gets, so the result holds those trees. A leaf is tested the same
way and read as the scalar it holds, so
`cards | map(c => c.metadata.pages) | somes | of_type(Int)` is the page counts
as a `List<Int>`, ready to sum.

| | `is(U)` | `of_type(U)` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Input | one value | a collection, or an `Option` |
| Result | `Bool` | the input's container, holding the `U`s, at element type `U` |
| Longhand | a two-arm `match` | `filter(x => x \| is(U))` |
| Narrows | no | yes |

The abstraction tree is whole:

```text
match   →  is
filter  →  of_type, by way of is
```

The rule that admits a shorthand is that its longhand can be typed.

## Building another container: a constructor

Interpretation never builds a value. Building one is construction: the
constructor of a concrete collection type takes the elements of its input.

```hql
List(xs)          // the positions of a Seq, materialized
xs | Set          // the distinct values, Set(xs)
```

- `List` takes a `Seq<T>`. It refuses a `Set<T>`, whose iteration order is
  unspecified, and names `sort`; and an open `Stream<T>`, which may not end,
  and names `take`.
- `Set` takes any `Collection<T>` and drops duplicates under HQL equality.
- Over a value already of the type, each is an identity and copies nothing.
- A constructor is a pipeline step: `x | C` is `C(x)`. There is no `list` or
  `set` beside `List` and `Set`.

Construction changes the container and keeps the elements. Turning a `String`
into an `Int` is parsing, not construction.

## Asserting: a binding

A program may also state a type without asking:

```hql
b : Book = [[dune]].metadata
```

This is not interpretation. It is a claim for the checker to prove from what it
already knows, and it fails at check time when it cannot. This is the "failed
narrowing" [[type-system]] calls a type error: something was written that is
not the type. Asking never produces that error, because asking always has
somewhere for the other case to go.

A binding to a supertype widens, and widening needs no step: a value of a
subtype is usable wherever its supertype is expected, so
`s : Seq<Hit<Card>> = ranking` is proven at once and the value is unchanged.
A binding never converts: `xs : List<Card> = seq` is refused, and the program
writes `List(seq)` where the list is built.

There is no `validate`, no `as`, no cast and no single-value `of_type`: each
would be a second spelling of a `match` arm, of a binding, or of a
constructor.

## Where it sits

```text
match x { U u => … }        the primitive: one value, every case named
x | is(U)                    the test: one value, a Bool
xs | of_type(U)              the selection: many values, the U case kept
x : U = …                    the assertion: proven or refused by the checker
List(xs), Set(xs)            construction: a new container, the same elements
```

`is`, the narrowing `filter`, `of_type` and the type arm of `match` are
implemented, with the nominal test for declared values and the structural
test for `Data` leaves and sequences of leaves. A record schema such as `Book`
waits for declared record types to be members of the lattice, the roadblock
"record and pair values" of
[issue 0016](../../issues/0016-sql-parity-analysis.md); until then a tree is
read as a scalar, a `Seq` of scalars or `Data`. The
constructors are implemented; `List` still accepts a `Set`, and a constructor
is not yet a pipeline step. See [[data-type]] for the tree being interpreted, [[doc-type]] for the
document whose header it is, [[option-type]] for the `none` the card example
returns, and [[hql/design-status]] for what remains undecided.
